findings.evidence:
sliceMapToSlice (openapi3filter/req_resp_decoder.go:936-962) turns the map keys of a decoded deepObject into a dense slice:
    for k := range m { key, err := strconv.Atoi(k); ...; keys = append(keys, key) }
    max := -1
    for _, k := range keys { if k > max { max = k } }
    for i := 0; i <= max; i++ { ... result = append(result, ...) }
max comes straight from the request. buildResObj (line 972-996) then does `resultArr := make([]any, len(arr))`, doubling it.
Reachability: urlValuesDecoder.DecodeObject case "deepObject" (line 661-687) builds props from the bracketed key segments -> makeObject (line 911) -> deepSet -> buildResObj -> array branch -> sliceMapToSlice.
PoC: for a parameter `p` (in: query, style: deepObject, explode: true) whose schema is `type: object` with a property `a` of `type: array`, the request `GET /x?p[a][2000000000]=y` drives max=2000000000 and allocates ~32 GB across the two slices. `?p[a][9223372036854775807]=y` never terminates. Neither maxItems nor maxLength is consulted, because decoding happens before openapi3.Schema.VisitJSON.

openapi3/schema.go:2654-2666:
  if !settings.patternValidationDisabled && schema.Pattern != "" {
      cpiface, _ := compiledPatterns.Load(schema.Pattern)
      cp, _ := cpiface.(RegexMatcher)
      if cp == nil {
          var err error
          if cp, err = schema.compilePattern(settings.regexCompiler); err != nil {
              if !settings.multiError { return err }
              me = append(me, err)      // error recorded, cp stays nil, execution continues
          }
      }
      if !cp.MatchString(value) {      // nil interface dereference
compilePattern (openapi3/schema_pattern.go:16-32) returns a nil RegexMatcher on failure. openapi3filter/options.go:26 MultiError is translated into openapi3.MultiErrors() at openapi3filter/validate_request.go:341 and validate_response.go:65, so the panicking combination is reachable through the standard filter and middleware entry points. The uncompilable pattern reaches request time either because doc.Validate was never called, or because it was called with openapi3.DisablePatternValidation() (openapi3/schema.go:1684 skips the compile check), which is the documented workaround for exactly these patterns.

openapi3/schema.go:2563-2569:
  if v := schema.MultipleOf; v != nil {
      numRat, denRat := &big.Rat{}, &big.Rat{}
      numRat.SetString(fmt.Sprintf("%.10f", value))
      denRat.SetString(fmt.Sprintf("%.10f", *v))
      if !(&big.Rat{}).Quo(numRat, denRat).IsInt() {
big.Rat.Quo panics when the divisor is zero. Grep for MultipleOf across openapi3/*.go (excluding tests) shows it only in the struct field (schema.go:116), marshalling (561), JSONLookup (852), IsEmpty (1276) and this check (2563) — Schema.validate (openapi3/schema.go:1396-1900) never rejects a non-positive multipleOf, even though JSON Schema requires it to be strictly greater than zero. The %.10f rounding is also a silent correctness bug: multipleOf 0.0000000001234 is evaluated as 0.0000000001.

openapi3filter/req_resp_decoder.go:662-681, inside the loop over every query parameter of the request:
  for key, values := range params {
      if !regexp.MustCompile(fmt.Sprintf(`^%s\[`, regexp.QuoteMeta(param))).MatchString(key) { continue }
      matches := regexp.MustCompile(`\[(.*?)\]`).FindAllStringSubmatch(key, -1)
Neither pattern depends on `key`, so both are recompiled for every iteration. The second is a constant. The follow-on loop at lines 704-719 is additionally O(len(schema.Properties) x len(props) x path depth) over the same attacker-sized input.

openapi3/schema_jsonschema_validator.go:204-212:
  func (schema *Schema) useJSONSchema2020(settings *schemaValidationSettings, value any) error {
      validator, err := newJSONSchemaValidator(schema)
      if err != nil {
          // Fall back to built-in validator if compilation fails
          return schema.visitJSON(settings, value)
      }
The compile input is json.Marshal(schema) (line 21). SchemaRef.MarshalYAML (openapi3/refs.go:1049-1054) emits `{"$ref": ...}` whenever Ref is non-empty, and the loader keeps Ref populated after resolution. The marshalled fragment is registered standalone as "https://example.com/schema.json" (lines 39-45), so an internal pointer such as "#/components/schemas/Node" has no target inside the resource and Compile returns an error — i.e. the fallback fires for ordinary 3.1 documents that use $ref. Confirming the built-in validator's gap: grepping schema.go shows PrefixItems/Contains/PatternProperties/DependentSchemas/PropertyNames/UnevaluatedProperties/If/Then/Else appear only in the struct, marshalling, JSONLookup, IsEmpty and the OAS-3.0 rejection list; visitJSONObject (2831-2998) checks only properties, min/maxProperties, additionalProperties, required, and visitJSONArray (2732-2824) only min/maxItems, uniqueItems, items. No test under openapi3/ exercises 2020-12 validation against a schema containing a $ref.

openapi3filter/validation_handler.go:47-49, inside Load():
  if h.AuthenticationFunc == nil {
      h.AuthenticationFunc = NoopAuthenticationFunc
  }
NoopAuthenticationFunc (openapi3filter/validation_handler.go:18) is `func(context.Context, *AuthenticationInput) error { return nil }`. Contrast openapi3filter/validate_request.go:431-434, where the same missing function yields ErrAuthenticationServiceMissing and the request is rejected. Load() also silently substitutes http.DefaultServeMux for a nil Handler (lines 44-46).

routers/gorillamux/router.go:52-75:
  servers, err := makeServers(doc.Servers)
  ...
  for _, path := range doc.Paths.InMatchingOrder() {
      pathItem := doc.Paths.Value(path)
      if len(pathItem.Servers) > 0 {
          if servers, err = makeServers(pathItem.Servers); err != nil { ... }   // assigns the OUTER servers
      }
      ...
      for _, s := range servers { muxRoute := muxRouter.Path(s.base + path)... }
The assignment targets the loop-invariant variable, so the override is never restored. Paths are iterated in a deterministic order (InMatchingOrder), so the corruption is stable rather than intermittent. Separately, makeServers dereferences `server.Variables[sVar]` and `server.Variables[portVariable]` (lines 135 and 160) without checking the map entry exists; NewRouter's doc comment assumes a .Validate()d document, but a caller who skips validation gets a nil-map-entry panic here.

openapi3/schema.go:2400-2407:
  if requireInteger {
      f, ok := settings.integerFormats[format]
      if !ok { f, ok = SchemaIntegerFormats[format] }
      if ok { if err := f.Validate(int64(value)); err != nil {
value is a float64. The conversion int64(value) is performed BEFORE the range check, and Go leaves out-of-range float-to-int conversions implementation-defined; on amd64 1e30 becomes math.MinInt64. The int64 validator registered at openapi3/schema_formats.go:56 is NewRangeFormatValidator(math.MinInt64, math.MaxInt64) — the full domain — so the clamped value always passes. The integer-ness gate ahead of it (line 2375, big.NewFloat(value).IsInt()) is satisfied because 1e30 is an exact float64 integer. Related precision loss: JSONBodyDecoder uses json.Number (req_resp_decoder.go:1363-1364) but visitJSON converts it via value.Float64() (schema.go:1997), so ids above 2^53 are silently rounded before any check runs.

openapi3/schema.go:2213-2216 and 2276-2279:
  if settings.asreq || settings.asrep { tempValue = deepCopyJSONValue(value) }
inside the per-branch loops, with deepCopyJSONValue (schema.go:3171-3190) recursively duplicating maps and slices. oneOf cannot short-circuit (it must count matches, lines 2203-2227) so it always pays k copies plus k traversals, then re-runs the matched branch on the original value at line 2250. anyOf repeats the matched branch at line 2299. No depth or node-count limit exists anywhere in visitJSON.
